{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794611176,"nonce":"d++DFdV75P30IHWBQx6tGopq6wc=","message":"Landed in e5ff3ac7b51e18757c1e63b99f7fc64ab1cf5d5a, with a window check in e1e755df. Open leftovers are ff66023 (opacity) and 0623714 (transitions), and the mobile runners are efa5528.\n\nA reading of the measurements for whoever picks this up: per frame the render phase is about 2.6 times cheaper on rows (1.06 ms against 3.0 ms in the window trace), steady CPU at a fixed frame rate is 15 to 25% lower, and the large difference is memory, 91 MB against 911 MB for 140 cards. Uncapped, rows draw at the display's 120 fps where the baked path managed 44, so total CPU is about 20% higher for 2.7 times the frames.\n\nTwo things found on the way. The first version woke the event loop on every driven value, which kept it spinning and cost five times the CPU of the baked path at 30 fps; drive_playing does not wake. And a driven value is borrowed mutably only when a binding or a value is added, because each mutable borrow of the bindings map counts as a change and invalidates the row plan: with a borrow every frame every frame was a full paint.","files":null}]}